  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 335 PM EDT MON OCT 5 2026\n\nThe period is expected to begin with an upper level ridge centered \noff the southwest U.S coast, with an upper low centered in the \nHudson Bay vicinity and a trough extending into the Upper MS Valley \nand parts of the Great Lakes. At the surface, a ridge of high \npressure should extend from the Southern Appalachians into the \nAtlantic, while a sfc front in advance of troughing extending into \nthe Upper MS Valley and Great Lakes should extend into northern MI \nto the Central Plains. Meanwhile, a lingering frontal zone should \nextend across portions of the Gulf. \n\nWednesday to Thursday night, the upper low in Canada should become \ncentered in portions of Quebec, while the associated shortwave \ntrough moves across the Great Lakes Wednesday and Wednesday night \nand across portions of the OH Valley on Thursday. Further west, \nupper ridging should become centered in the Pacific to the west and \nsouthwest of Baja, with ridging continuing to extend into the western \nConus. Downstream, by late Thursday night, a 500 mb trough axis is \nanticipated from Quebec into the eastern Great Lakes to Central to \nSouthern Appalachians. Guidance generally also has a weak upper low \nin or near TX by Thursday night and guidance (especially GFS) also \npoints toward possible tropical system formation potentially as \nearly as this time frame or at least a sfc low somewhere in the \ncentral to southwestern Gulf. Sfc low pressure in advance of the \nupper low in Canada and its associated shortwave should move across \nthe Great Lakes and Northeast, but the southern end will likely \nbecome ill-defined or diffuse as it approaches the OH Valley. A \nridge of sfc high pressure should generally remain through this \nperiod in the southern to central Appalachians with a trend of 500 \nmb height rises over the northern portions of the Gulf states into \nthe TN Valley and Southern Appalachians by later Thursday night.\n\nFor eastern KY, the pattern should favor a moderation in high \ntemperatures from Wednesday to Thursday and a ridge/valley \ntemperature split for Wednesday night and probably Thursday night as \nwell. Overnight lows should tend to moderate as sfc dewpoints inch \nupward compared to earlier in the week, but valley fog should \ncontinue to be a feature each night with ridging generally \ndominating. The weakening front and passing shortwave could lead to \na bit more cloud cover later Wednesday night into Thursday morning \nthan currently reflected in sky grids.\n\nFriday to Saturday night, a narrow upper ridge axis that shifts \nnorth is progged by recent GFS and ECMWF runs from the Carolinas \nacross the southern Appalachians to near the confluence of the OH \nand TN Rivers for Friday to Friday night while an upper low becomes \ncentered in the Arklatex vicinity by later Friday night or early \nSaturday. At that point, moisture should begin to or have spread to \nnear the Gulf Coast or into the Gulf coast states and eventually \nnorth into the Southern Appalachians and TN Valley and possibly into \nthe Lower OH Valley by later Saturday or Saturday night pending the \nevolution of a tropical system in the Gulf while the upper low may \nmove to near the mid MS Valley vicinity by late Saturday night. \nGuidance has uncertainty with the strength of this system with the \n12Z operational GFS run stronger than the 06Z GFS run or the last two \noperational ECMWF runs. \n\nAcross eastern KY this scenario would bring continued mild high \ntemperatures and increasing cloud cover by Saturday or Saturday \nnight into the end of the weekend and increasing moisture and the \npossible approach of the upper system into the OH Valley could \nresult in showers by late Saturday night, though better chances \nappear to be early next week.\n\nSunday to Monday, the upper low or trough that much of the guidance \nhas reaching the mid MS Valley late Sat night or early Sun and then \ntracks it across the OH Valley region though Sun night or Mon with \nupper ridging and height rises around or near the end of the period. \nAs previously mentioned this leads to higher chances for showers to \nend the weekend, though model spread remains and how far north \nappreciable moisture gets may also change. This timeframe may be our \nnext reasonable chance for measurable rainfall, but timing and \namounts are highly uncertain.",
